-This page fixes the constant-versus-balanced Deutsch promise, the $n$-bit Deutsch–Jozsa promise, and the no-offset Bernstein–Vazirani promise. It defines the exact one-query circuits with their $X$ and Hadamard preparation gates and proves the measured output laws from the finite $\mathbb F_2^n$ Hadamard character sum. The classical results distinguish deterministic exact query complexity from randomized bounded-error cost; in particular, the Deutsch–Jozsa exact deterministic lower bound is not a bounded-error exponential lower bound because three distinct random samples already achieve success at least $3/4$ ([[thm-qa-deutsch-classical-query-lower-bound]], [[thm-qa-deutsch-jozsa-deterministic-query-lower-bound]], [[thm-qa-deutsch-jozsa-randomized-constant-query-upper-bound]], [[thm-qa-bernstein-vazirani-classical-query-lower-bound]]).
+This page fixes the constant-versus-balanced Deutsch promise, the Deutsch–Jozsa promise on $n$-bit addresses (encoded by an $N=2^n$-bit truth table), and the no-offset Bernstein–Vazirani promise. It defines the exact one-query circuits with their $X$ and Hadamard preparation gates and proves the measured output laws from the finite $\mathbb F_2^n$ Hadamard character sum. The classical results distinguish deterministic exact query complexity from randomized bounded-error cost; in particular, the Deutsch–Jozsa exact deterministic lower bound is not a bounded-error exponential lower bound because three distinct random samples already achieve success at least $3/4$ ([[thm-qa-deutsch-classical-query-lower-bound]], [[thm-qa-deutsch-jozsa-deterministic-query-lower-bound]], [[thm-qa-deutsch-jozsa-randomized-constant-query-upper-bound]], [[thm-qa-bernstein-vazirani-classical-query-lower-bound]]).
